 "transcript": {
  "answer": "Yes, it's getting somewhat harder. The change is gradual, and Queensland is still doing a bit better than the national average.\n\n**Unemployment rate (seasonally adjusted)**\n- Queensland was at **4.53% in September 2026**. That's up from 3.96% in January 2025 and 4.17% in September 2025.\n- The rate has risen in each of the last three months: 4.24% in July, 4.51% in August and 4.53% in September.\n- Australia was at **4.72%** in September 2026, up from 4.13% in January 2025. It has risen faster than Queensland's rate recently.\n\n**Number of people unemployed in Queensland**\n- About **144,400** in September 2026, compared with about 130,600 a year earlier and 122,800 in January 2025.\n- That's roughly 14,000 more than a year ago.\n\n**Jobs**\n- Employment is still growing in absolute terms, to about 3.043 million in September 2026, up about 38,000 on a year earlier.\n- The labour force has grown faster, by about 51,000 over the same year. Job growth isn't keeping up with the number of people looking for work.\n- The employment-to-population ratio fell to **63.9%** in September 2026, from 64.3% a year earlier and 64.6% in January 2025.\n- Participation is steady at about 66.9–67.0%. The rise in unemployment comes from jobs not keeping pace with population growth, not from more people joining the workforce.\n\n**Caveat:** Queensland's monthly figures are volatile. The rate swung between 3.7% and 4.4% in the first half of 2026, for example. A single month isn't conclusive. The multi-month pattern of a rising unemployment rate and a falling employment ratio is more reliable, and it matches the national picture.",
